WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.690
Last week, the billionaire
businessman Richard Branson

00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:03.936
hurled himself to the edge of space

00:00:03.936 --> 00:00:06.219
on a ship he helped
privately fund and develop,

00:00:06.219 --> 00:00:08.080
and came back smiling.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:09.594
Now Jeff Bezos is doing the same.

00:00:09.594 --> 00:00:12.730
Billionaires launching themselves
skyward on their own dime

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:15.404
has occasioned a bitter debate
about income inequality,

00:00:15.404 --> 00:00:17.562
whether the government should
be taking more of their wealth

00:00:17.562 --> 00:00:18.957
through taxation.

00:00:18.957 --> 00:00:21.457
Here on earth, in the richest
country on the planet,

00:00:21.457 --> 00:00:23.421
half our people live paycheck to paycheck,

00:00:23.421 --> 00:00:25.177
complained Bernie Sanders,

00:00:25.177 --> 00:00:26.707
But hey, the richest guys in the world

00:00:26.707 --> 00:00:28.387
are off in outer space.

00:00:28.387 --> 00:00:31.039
Yes. It's time to tax the billionaires.

00:00:31.039 --> 00:00:34.870
The anger directed at Bezos
Branson, and SpaceX's Elon Musk

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:36.827
stands in striking contrast
to the high approval

00:00:36.827 --> 00:00:38.600
long enjoyed by NASA,

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:39.489
even though the space agency

00:00:39.489 --> 00:00:41.550
spent the better part of the last decade

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:44.020
unable to get humans off-planet at all,

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:46.668
while still soaking up
billions in taxpayer dollars.

00:00:46.668 --> 00:00:49.564
Should billionaires play out
their space travel fantasies,

00:00:49.564 --> 00:00:53.150
tweeted Congressman Ro Kahanna,
a member of the NASA Caucus,

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:56.097
or should we invest in
schooling, provide healthcare,

00:00:56.097 --> 00:00:57.930
and create prosperity for everyone?

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:01.005
The irony is that NASA takes
our money without our consent

00:01:01.005 --> 00:01:02.281
to finance a space program

00:01:02.281 --> 00:01:05.282
that no ordinary citizen
can ever hope to access.

00:01:05.282 --> 00:01:08.403
But when Branson, Musk, and
Bezos spend their wealth

00:01:08.403 --> 00:01:11.119
with the explicit goal of
selling ever cheaper tickets

00:01:11.119 --> 00:01:12.225
to all comers,

00:01:12.225 --> 00:01:14.074
that's when congressmen get grumpy.

00:01:14.074 --> 00:01:15.620
There's every reason to believe

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:17.602
the democratization of
space travel is upon us.

00:01:17.602 --> 00:01:19.310
Bezos, Branson, and Musk

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:21.720
have already delivered on
that promise in other sectors,

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.793
unless the government
manages to screw things up.

00:01:23.793 --> 00:01:26.030
Following the pattern
of commercial air travel

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:27.200
in the 20th century,

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:28.750
a novelty for billionaires today

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:30.967
may well be accessible
to the ordinary rich,

00:01:30.967 --> 00:01:33.066
and then the middle class soon enough.

00:01:33.066 --> 00:01:35.868
The billionaires also say
they're taking a long-term view.

00:01:35.868 --> 00:01:38.919
One day, the capacity to get
off planet cheaply and at scale

00:01:38.919 --> 00:01:40.820
could be humanity's salvation.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:43.546
But what they want or envision
isn't what really matters.

00:01:43.546 --> 00:01:45.881
Just as bubble wrap was
invented to be wallpaper

00:01:45.881 --> 00:01:47.683
and Listerine was for cleaning floors,

00:01:47.683 --> 00:01:49.430
consumers will decide

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:51.800
where the private space
industry ultimately leads.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:54.700
Bezos, Branson, Musk,
and others have overtaken

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:58.118
a wildly expensive,
ineffective government program

00:01:58.118 --> 00:01:59.829
and built a competitive industry,

00:01:59.829 --> 00:02:02.100
driving down the cost
of getting a kilogram

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.894
into Low Earth Orbit by 44 fold already.

00:02:04.894 --> 00:02:07.894
Which billionaire goes to
space first, how high he flies,

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:09.670
or how big his rocket is,

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:11.902
or how much he pays in income taxes,

00:02:11.902 --> 00:02:13.341
none of that matters.

00:02:13.341 --> 00:02:15.700
What matters is what the
rest of us are gonna do

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.530
with access to those same spacecraft

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:22.030
and bigger, better, weirder
ones in the years to come.

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:26.823
Woo, space!

